# testing/org-tokens.local — YOUR organization's identifiers, one per line.
#
# Copy this file to `testing/org-tokens.local` (which is gitignored, so the
# tokens never ship) and fill it in. `skill_lint.py` then fails on any shipped
# file that contains one of them, case-insensitively, as a substring.
#
#     cp testing/org-tokens.local.example testing/org-tokens.local
#     $EDITOR testing/org-tokens.local
#     python3 testing/skill_lint.py
#
# Why the file is not committed: a denylist that ships would put the very strings
# it forbids into a public repo. The cost is that it protects nothing until you
# create it — CI never has it, and for one full release cycle it existed on no
# machine at all, which is how "per <Team> convention" reached main. The
# shape-based checks (`org-signature`, `example-locale`, `example-keys`, and the
# generic half of `org-data`) cover that class without this file; the tokens here
# are for everything a shape cannot describe.
#
# Rule: whenever you purge an identifier from the tree, add it here in the SAME
# commit. That is what stops it from coming back.
#
# Below are the categories worth listing, with fictional values — replace them.

# --- team, squad, department, and product code names ---------------------------
Zorg
Zorgtech
Zorg Platform Squad

# --- Jira project / board keys, Confluence space keys --------------------------
ZORG
ZORGX

# --- hosts and domains that are yours ------------------------------------------
zorgtech.example
wiki.zorgtech.example

# --- instance identifiers (Atlassian cloud id, Jira team id, workspace id) -----
00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# --- real people: surnames of colleagues who appear in your local context ------
Surname
Anothername

# --- internal tools and services nobody outside your org would recognize -------
Zorgboard
